 an-5x-playing-wretched-in-the-candyman-films-of-bernard-rose-and-nia-dacos
 ta-zayla-crocker-phd-candidate-unc-chapel-hill\n\nSituated in Black media 
 studies and play studies\, Zayla Crocker examines Bernard Rose’s 1992 fi
 lm Candyman along with Nia DaCosta’s remake of the same name to demonstr
 ate how both films encourage the spectator to “play” within the genre 
 conventions of the slasher film through an act of ritualized play Crocker 
 deems “playing wretched.” Both films overtly or covertly prompt the sp
 ectator to call on and summon the performance of Black memory\, history\, 
 and trauma in the figure of a hook-handed slasher villain. As a whole\, Cr
 ocker argues that the affective performance of horror and play acts as a m
 emorial vehicle that situates the memory of Black trauma within the lived 
 reality of the spectator. Outside of the film\, spectators can join the ga
 me\, and they too can call on Candyman and summon the memory of the other.
 \n\nZayla Crocker is a PhD Candidate and teaching fellow in the English De
 partment at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Her academic 
 research focuses on the liberatory potential of the horror genre with an e
 mphasis on media theory and cultural trauma through novels\, films\, and v
 ideo games. Currently\, her research is centered on Black expressions of t
 rauma in the horror genre with a cross-media exploration of memory and aff
 ect.\n\nVISITOR INFORMATION\nOshman Hall is located in the McMurtry Buildi
 ng on Stanford campus at 355 Roth Way. Visitor parking is free after 4pm o
 n weekdays\, except by the Oval. Alternatively\, take the Caltrain to Palo
